    "q-134506": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134506",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{4}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{4}} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16\\cdot p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{3}\\cdot p} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-4\\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+p\\sqrt[3]{p} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{p}-4\\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+p\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ (2-4)\\sqrt{p}+(1+p)\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt{p}+(1+p)\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ (p+1)\\sqrt[3]{p}-2\\sqrt{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot combine the $\\sqrt[3]{p}$ and $\\sqrt{p}$ terms.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
